@* The day-over-month block a recorded game is listed under: "14" with "MAR" beneath it, and the
   full date on hover, since a row has no room for the year.

   The games list and the dashboard's attention panel both draw one, and both must read the date
   back exactly as stored rather than converting it: StartedAt carries the wall clock the player
   wrote down, at offset zero, and shifting it to server-local time moves a late-evening game onto
   the wrong day. Two copies of that were two chances to convert one of them. *@

<div class="@(Small ? "w-10" : "w-11") shrink-0 text-center" title="@GameFormat.LongDate(Date)">
    <div class="numeral-display @(Small ? "text-base" : "text-lg") text-ink-900">
        @Date.ToString("dd")
    </div>
    <div class="mt-0.5 micro-label">@Date.ToString("MMM")</div>
</div>

@code {
    /// <summary>The game's start, already unwrapped from its offset — see the note above.</summary>
    [Parameter, EditorRequired] public DateTime Date { get; set; }

    /// <summary>A step down, for the dashboard's two-column panel where a full-width row's
    /// numeral is too heavy.</summary>
    [Parameter] public bool Small { get; set; }
}
